The base case of a recursive function is when the function is called recursively, and if it is, it returns the result of calling itself. This means that if the function is called recursively, it will eventually return the original function.

To implement recursion, we can define a recursive function called `recursive` that takes an input `n` and returns the result of calling it recursively. The base case is when `n` is 0 or 1, in which case the function returns 1.

